# VyapaarPeCharcha

> Voice-note ERP for India's oral-first wholesalers, kiranas, and small distributors.

## 1. Product brief

VyapaarPeCharcha converts a merchant's natural, code-mixed voice note into a reviewable business transaction: customer, products, quantities, delivery date, price, outstanding balance, inventory movement, invoice, and ledger entry.

The product is designed for businesses where orders currently live in conversations, WhatsApp voice notes, notebooks, and memory. The core promise is simple:

**Speak once. Review once. The ledger updates itself.**

### Primary user

- A wholesaler, kirana owner, or small distributor who works mainly from a phone.
- Communicates in Hindi, English, or code-mixed Indic speech.
- Tracks informal credit and repeat customers.
- Has a small SKU catalog but inconsistent product naming.
- Needs value immediately and will not complete ERP training or lengthy setup.

### MVP success criteria

- A merchant can create an order from a voice note in under 30 seconds.
- The system extracts required order fields into validated JSON.
- Ambiguous or missing fields trigger a focused clarification, not a silent guess.
- No inventory, invoice, or ledger mutation occurs before merchant confirmation.
- Confirming once creates the order, stock movement, invoice, and ledger entry atomically.
- The merchant receives a readable and optional spoken confirmation in their preferred language.

## 2. Scope

### Hackathon MVP

1. Merchant and customer setup.
2. SKU catalog with aliases such as `Sprite peti`, `स्प्राइट crate`, and `Sprite case`.
3. Voice-note upload or in-browser recording.
4. Indic/code-mixed transcription through Sarvam Speech-to-Text.
5. Structured order extraction through Sarvam-105B.
6. Confidence checks and one-question-at-a-time clarification.
7. Review-and-confirm screen.
8. Atomic order, inventory, invoice, and credit-ledger creation.
9. PDF invoice generation.
10. Local-language text confirmation and optional Bulbul voice confirmation.
11. Bill/invoice reconciliation proof of concept through Sarvam Vision.

### Explicitly out of scope for the MVP

- GST filing, e-invoicing, or statutory accounting certification.
- Full double-entry accounting.
- Automated bank settlement or payment collection.
- Multi-warehouse optimization.
- WhatsApp Business production integration.
- Autonomous mutations without merchant confirmation.

## 3. Experience flow

Example input:

> “Ramesh ko kal ke liye 6 peti Sprite, 4 Coke, aur 20 Limca bhejna. Uska last ₹12,500 pending hai.”

Expected flow:

1. The app records and uploads the voice note.
2. Speech-to-Text returns the transcript and detected language.
3. The extraction service resolves `Ramesh`, matches catalog aliases, normalizes quantities, and parses `kal` using the merchant's timezone.
4. The policy engine marks uncertain fields and checks stock and credit context.
5. If `4 Coke` lacks a unit, the app asks: “Coke ki 4 peti ya 4 bottles?”
6. The merchant reviews the final order summary.
7. Confirmation triggers one transactional write across order, inventory, invoice, and ledger.
8. The app responds: “Order #VL-1042 ban gaya. Delivery kal. Ramesh ka total pending ₹18,940.”

## 4. System architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    U[Merchant PWA] -->|audio + merchant context| API[Application API]
    API --> OBJ[(Object storage)]
    API --> JOB[Workflow orchestrator]

    JOB --> STT[Sarvam Speech-to-Text\nSaaras]
    STT --> NORM[Transcript normalizer]
    NORM --> LLM[Sarvam-105B\nstructured extraction]
    LLM --> RES[Entity resolver\ncustomers + SKU aliases]
    RES --> POL[Validation and policy engine]

    POL -->|needs clarification| API
    POL -->|reviewable draft| API
    U -->|confirm| CMD[Transactional command service]

    CMD --> DB[(PostgreSQL)]
    CMD --> INV[Invoice renderer]
    INV --> OBJ
    CMD --> OUT[Outbox events]

    OUT --> TTS[Bulbul TTS]
    OUT --> NOTIFY[In-app notification]

    U -->|bill image or PDF| DOC[Sarvam Vision]
    DOC --> RECON[Reconciliation service]
    RECON --> POL
```

### Architectural principle

AI proposes; deterministic services validate; the merchant confirms; the database commits.

The LLM is never the source of truth for prices, balances, stock, customer identity, tax rules, or dates. It produces a typed proposal referencing canonical IDs. All financial and inventory effects are calculated and committed by application code.

### Component responsibilities

| Component | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Merchant PWA | Record audio, show transcript, collect clarification, review order, work on low-end Android browsers. |
| Application API | Authentication, signed uploads, request validation, rate limiting, and client-facing workflow state. |
| Workflow orchestrator | Durable STT/extraction jobs, retries, timeouts, idempotency, and state transitions. |
| Sarvam STT adapter | Transcribe code-mixed audio and retain provider metadata without leaking provider details into domain logic. |
| Extraction service | Prompt Sarvam-105B for schema-constrained business intents and evidence spans. |
| Entity resolver | Match spoken customer/product names against merchant-scoped canonical records and aliases. |
| Policy engine | Check completeness, confidence, date plausibility, credit context, SKU units, price rules, and stock. |
| Transactional command service | Commit confirmed order, line items, inventory movements, invoice, and ledger entry in one transaction. |
| Document reconciliation | Extract supplier bills with Sarvam Vision and compare them with purchase/inventory records. |
| Notification service | Produce local-language text and optional Bulbul speech after a successful commit. |

## 5. Sarvam integration design

All Sarvam calls sit behind internal interfaces so model or endpoint changes require an adapter update rather than a domain rewrite.

```text
SpeechRecognizer.transcribe(audio, languageHint, mode) -> Transcript
BusinessIntentExtractor.extract(transcript, merchantContext) -> DraftCommand
DocumentExtractor.extract(document, schema) -> ExtractedDocument
Translator.translate(text, source, target) -> string
SpeechSynthesizer.speak(text, language, voice) -> AudioAsset
```

### Recommended configuration

| Capability | Initial choice | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Voice transcription | `saaras:v3`, `mode=codemix` | Pin explicitly. Keep `saaras:v4` behind a feature flag until enabled and regression-tested for the account. |
| Structured reasoning | `sarvam-105b` | Use low temperature, a strict JSON schema, catalog context, and evidence spans. |
| Customer-facing dialogue | `sarvam-105b-conversations` | Optional; deterministic clarification templates are safer for MVP. |
| Document extraction | `sarvam-vision` | Use for supplier bills and invoice line-item extraction. |
| Text translation | `sarvam-translate` | Use only when UI text must be rendered in another supported language. |
| Spoken confirmation | `bulbul:v3` | Convert mixed-language output to native script before TTS where possible. |

### Runtime configuration

```dotenv
SARVAM_API_KEY=server-side-secret
SARVAM_STT_MODEL=saaras:v3
SARVAM_STT_MODE=codemix
SARVAM_LLM_MODEL=sarvam-105b
SARVAM_TTS_MODEL=bulbul:v3
SARVAM_VISION_MODEL=sarvam-vision
SARVAM_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
SARVAM_MAX_RETRIES=2
```

Never ship the Sarvam key to the browser. Store request IDs, latency, model ID, and error category for observability, but redact audio URLs, transcripts, phone numbers, and financial values from general logs.

## 6. Core data model

```mermaid
erDiagram
    MERCHANT ||--o{ USER : has
    MERCHANT ||--o{ CUSTOMER : serves
    MERCHANT ||--o{ SKU : stocks
    CUSTOMER ||--o{ SALES_ORDER : places
    SALES_ORDER ||--|{ ORDER_ITEM : contains
    SKU ||--o{ ORDER_ITEM : referenced_by
    SALES_ORDER ||--o| INVOICE : produces
    CUSTOMER ||--o{ LEDGER_ENTRY : owns
    SKU ||--o{ INVENTORY_MOVEMENT : affects
    VOICE_JOB ||--o| SALES_ORDER : creates
    VOICE_JOB ||--o{ CLARIFICATION : requires
```

### Essential tables

- `merchants`: name, timezone, default language, invoice settings.
- `users`: merchant role, phone/email identity, session metadata.
- `customers`: canonical name, aliases, phone, address, credit limit.
- `skus`: canonical label, spoken aliases, base unit, units-per-case, selling price, tax metadata.
- `voice_jobs`: audio key, transcript, language, provider request ID, state, prompt version.
- `draft_commands`: extracted JSON, evidence, confidence, validation errors, revision.
- `clarifications`: field path, question, answer, resolution status.
- `sales_orders` and `order_items`: confirmed commercial intent and pricing snapshot.
- `inventory_movements`: immutable stock deltas linked to a source order or purchase.
- `invoices`: invoice number, totals, status, PDF asset key.
- `ledger_entries`: immutable debit/credit events with running balance calculated by query or materialized view.
- `outbox_events`: reliable post-commit notification and TTS work.

### Draft command schema

```json
{
  "intent": "create_sales_order",
  "customer": {
    "spoken_name": "Ramesh",
    "candidate_id": "cus_123",
    "confidence": 0.96,
    "evidence": "Ramesh ko"
  },
  "delivery_date": {
    "value": "2026-09-06",
    "confidence": 0.99,
    "evidence": "kal ke liye"
  },
  "items": [
    {
      "spoken_name": "Sprite",
      "candidate_sku_id": "sku_sprite_24x250",
      "quantity": 6,
      "unit": "case",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "evidence": "6 peti Sprite"
    }
  ],
  "mentioned_previous_balance": 12500,
  "currency": "INR",
  "missing_fields": [],
  "warnings": []
}
```

Provider confidence alone must not authorize a mutation. For customer and SKU resolution, use a combined score from exact aliases, phonetic/fuzzy matching, recency, order history, and extraction evidence. Below-threshold matches remain unresolved.

## 7. Command and consistency model

### Workflow states

```text
UPLOADED -> TRANSCRIBING -> EXTRACTING -> VALIDATING
         -> NEEDS_CLARIFICATION -> VALIDATING
         -> READY_FOR_REVIEW -> CONFIRMED -> COMMITTED
         -> FAILED or CANCELLED
```

### Confirmation transaction

For a confirmed draft, the server must:

1. Lock the draft revision and reject stale confirmations.
2. Re-read canonical prices, stock, customer balance, and merchant settings.
3. Create the order and line items with a pricing snapshot.
4. Append inventory movements.
5. Create the invoice and ledger debit.
6. Append an outbox event.
7. Commit everything or roll back everything.

Use an idempotency key derived from `merchant_id + voice_job_id + confirmed_revision`. Retrying the command returns the original result rather than creating a duplicate order.

### Clarification rules

Ask only when the answer changes fulfillment or money. Typical triggers:

- Customer match is ambiguous.
- Quantity or unit is missing.
- Product maps to multiple pack sizes.
- Relative date is implausible or outside the supported horizon.
- Spoken balance disagrees materially with the system balance.
- Requested quantity exceeds stock and backorders are disabled.

Ask one compact question at a time and offer likely options as large tap targets.

## 8. Suggested implementation stack

| Layer | Hackathon choice | Production evolution |
|---|---|---|
| Client | Next.js PWA, TypeScript, MediaRecorder API | React Native wrapper if background recording and device integrations become essential. |
| API | Fastify or NestJS on Node.js/TypeScript | Split only when team or scaling boundaries justify it. |
| Database | PostgreSQL with Prisma or Drizzle | Add read replicas/materialized views for reporting. |
| Queue | Postgres-backed job queue | Move to managed Redis/SQS when throughput requires it. |
| Object storage | S3-compatible bucket with signed URLs | Lifecycle policies and regional replication. |
| Invoice | HTML/CSS template rendered to PDF | GST/e-invoice provider integration after compliance review. |
| Auth | OTP or passwordless magic link | Device binding, staff roles, and stronger recovery controls. |
| Observability | Structured events, traces, provider latency metrics | PII-aware error analytics, SLOs, and cost dashboards. |

A modular monolith is the correct starting point. The AI workflow, catalog, order, inventory, invoice, and ledger modules should have clear interfaces but deploy together for the MVP.

## 9. API surface

```http
POST   /v1/voice-jobs/uploads             # create signed upload
POST   /v1/voice-jobs                     # submit uploaded audio
GET    /v1/voice-jobs/{id}                # poll workflow state and draft
POST   /v1/voice-jobs/{id}/clarifications # answer one clarification
POST   /v1/voice-jobs/{id}/confirm        # commit reviewed revision
POST   /v1/voice-jobs/{id}/cancel

GET    /v1/customers?query=
POST   /v1/customers
GET    /v1/skus?query=
POST   /v1/skus
POST   /v1/skus/{id}/aliases

GET    /v1/orders/{id}
GET    /v1/invoices/{id}/pdf
GET    /v1/customers/{id}/ledger

POST   /v1/reconciliation-documents       # upload bill/invoice
GET    /v1/reconciliation-documents/{id}
POST   /v1/reconciliation-documents/{id}/apply
```

Mutating endpoints require `Idempotency-Key`. Responses use stable error codes such as `AMBIGUOUS_CUSTOMER`, `MISSING_UNIT`, `STALE_DRAFT`, and `INSUFFICIENT_STOCK` so the client does not parse prose.

## 10. Implementation plan

### Phase 0 — Foundation

- Create monorepo with `apps/web`, `apps/api`, and `packages/domain`.
- Add local PostgreSQL, migrations, seed merchant, customers, and 30 realistic SKUs.
- Define the draft-command JSON schema and workflow states.
- Add environment validation, health check, request IDs, and PII-safe logging.
- Record a small evaluation set of Hinglish order utterances.

**Exit:** seeded app runs locally; schema migration and CI checks pass.

### Phase 1 — Voice to reviewable order

- Build record/upload experience with duration and format checks.
- Implement signed object upload and `voice_job` creation.
- Add Sarvam STT adapter and normalized transcript storage.
- Add `sarvam-105b` extraction prompt with strict schema validation.
- Implement merchant-scoped customer/SKU resolver and aliases.
- Build review screen with transcript evidence and editable fields.

**Exit:** ten representative recordings produce reviewable drafts; failures are visible and retryable.

### Phase 2 — Clarify and commit

- Add deterministic validation and clarification rules.
- Implement clarification UI and draft revisioning.
- Implement idempotent confirmation transaction.
- Generate invoice PDF and expose ledger/order views.
- Add stock check and immutable inventory movement entries.

**Exit:** a confirmed draft creates exactly one consistent order, invoice, stock movement set, and ledger entry.

### Phase 3 — Local-language confirmation and bill demo

- Generate compact local-language confirmation text.
- Add Bulbul TTS with native-script normalization.
- Add Sarvam Vision bill upload and extracted line-item review.
- Reconcile extracted supplier items against SKU aliases and inventory receipts.
- Add the end-to-end demo fixture and reset script.

**Exit:** the hackathon demo completes voice order capture and one bill-reconciliation path without manual database edits.

### Phase 4 — Pilot hardening

- Test with 3–5 merchants across noisy counters, regional accents, and real catalogs.
- Add onboarding from spreadsheet/photo, pack-size aliases, and price overrides.
- Add offline-friendly draft recording and resumable uploads.
- Add staff roles, audit timeline, backups, retention controls, and account deletion.
- Measure extraction accuracy, clarification rate, confirmation latency, corrections, and provider cost.
- Complete GST/accounting and privacy review before claiming compliance.

## 11. Testing strategy

### Automated tests

- Unit tests for dates, units, currency, pack conversion, balance math, and state transitions.
- Contract tests against recorded Sarvam responses; live smoke tests run separately with a budget cap.
- Golden-set tests for extraction schema and entity resolution.
- Transaction tests proving rollback and idempotent retry behavior.
- Authorization tests enforcing merchant-level data isolation.
- Snapshot/PDF tests for invoice rendering.

### Voice evaluation set

Build at least 50 consented or synthetic utterances spanning:

- Hindi-English code mix and native-script output.
- Common brands, local nicknames, and similar SKU names.
- Units such as peti, crate, carton, dozen, piece, kilo, and pouch.
- Background fan/traffic/shop noise.
- Corrections: “6 nahi, 8 peti.”
- Relative dates and times.
- Informal credit statements and conflicting balances.

Track field-level precision/recall, exact order accuracy, clarification rate, unresolved entity rate, median time-to-review, and false-confirmation count. The last metric must remain zero in testing.

## 12. Security, privacy, and reliability

- Encrypt audio, transcripts, invoices, and database storage in transit and at rest.
- Use signed, short-lived object URLs and merchant-scoped authorization on every request.
- Set a clear retention period for raw audio and allow deletion after successful processing.
- Keep an immutable audit trail for draft edits, confirmation, cancellation, and reconciliation.
- Never place secrets or raw PII in client bundles, prompts logs, analytics, or exception traces.
- Validate MIME type, duration, size, and malware risk before processing uploads.
- Apply per-merchant rate limits and provider circuit breakers.
- Queue provider calls with bounded retries and exponential backoff; never retry a committed business command blindly.
- Treat uploaded documents and transcripts as untrusted data, not instructions.
- Display invoice/tax outputs as operational documents until reviewed for applicable Indian compliance requirements.

## 13. Observability and product metrics

### Operational

- STT, extraction, Vision, and TTS latency and error rates.
- Queue age, timeout rate, retry count, and provider cost per completed order.
- Transaction failures, duplicate-prevention hits, and notification failures.

### Product

- Voice jobs reaching confirmation.
- Median seconds from recording to confirmed order.
- Fields edited before confirmation.
- Clarification questions per order.
- SKU/customer auto-resolution rate.
- Daily active merchants and repeat voice-order rate.
- Ledger balance corrections and inventory discrepancy rate.

## 14. Demo script

1. Open a seeded merchant with customer Ramesh and a realistic beverage catalog.
2. Record the Hinglish example order.
3. Show the transcript, extracted order, evidence, current balance, and one intentional unit ambiguity.
4. Answer the clarification and confirm.
5. Show the generated invoice, reduced stock, and updated Ramesh ledger.
6. Play the local-language voice confirmation.
7. Upload a supplier bill and show Sarvam Vision proposing reconciled inventory lines.

Keep a pre-recorded audio file and cached provider responses as a demo fallback, clearly labeled as fallback mode.

## 15. Definition of done

- [ ] New merchant can be seeded or onboarded with customers and SKU aliases.
- [ ] Supported audio uploads create durable workflow jobs.
- [ ] Transcript and detected language are visible to the merchant.
- [ ] Extraction returns schema-valid data or a recoverable error.
- [ ] Ambiguity creates a targeted clarification.
- [ ] Review edits create a new immutable draft revision.
- [ ] Confirmation is atomic and idempotent.
- [ ] Invoice, inventory movements, and ledger entry reconcile exactly.
- [ ] Text and optional voice confirmation are delivered after commit.
- [ ] Bill extraction is reviewable before inventory reconciliation.
- [ ] Evaluation set, tests, demo reset, and failure fallback all pass.
- [ ] Raw audio retention, data deletion, and PII logging policy are documented.

